 {
  "author_association": "OWNER",
  "body": "Pushed `dd2f75f`, fixing the two findings from the 2026-09-26 review that are specific to this branch: #75 and #76. Both are in code this PR introduces, so they are fixed here rather than tracked as follow-up.\n\n**#76 \u2014 legacy watermark adoption (spending).** Adoption was guarded on whether *this* repository had a qualified row, and the legacy rows were deliberately left on disk for downgrade safety. On a shared store those combine badly: the rows belong to the one repository that was polling before the upgrade, so the second repository pointed at that file adopted a timestamp it had never polled and enqueued its entire backlog since. Adoption now runs once per store \u2014 only while no qualified row exists anywhere \u2014 and deletes the legacy rows in the same `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`, so two daemons starting together cannot both adopt. That costs a downgrade its watermark, documented in `STORAGE.md` as the cheaper side of the trade.\n\n**#75 \u2014 the budget role on SIGHUP.** `Governor` fixes `_comply` at construction and `resolve_budget`'s two-authorities refusal runs only at startup, so a reload could promote a complier over a live authority with no refusal, or demote an authority while the log reported the authority's limits were still in force. `authority` and `comply` now join `github`/`triggers`/`store` as settled at startup: limits still reload, the role is pinned, the disagreement is logged at `WARNING`.\n\nAll four new tests were verified as genuine regressions \u2014 each fails against `04f6420`.\n\nGate: **1011 passed, 2 skipped** \u00b7 ruff clean \u00b7 pyright **0 errors** \u00b7 pylint **9.95 (+0.00)**.\n\nThe other 25 issues from that review (#61\u2013#92) are untouched here; #68 and #61 in particular look worth prioritising, but they are not this branch's code.\n",
  "created_at": "2026-09-26T17:51:35Z",
